  "analysis": {
    "why_you_should_care": "It is a guided tour of everything a browser engine usually hides: layout, dirty rectangles, input, and the tax of doing it without a GC.",
    "what_happened": "A developer implemented UI rendering, a DOM-like tree, and networking hooks in C instead of using a conventional web stack.",
    "how_it_works": "Widgets are structs. Layout is a walk over a tree. Drawing hits a backend (often CPU). Networking is whatever sockets the author wrapped. There is no standard DOM; there is an ad-hoc tree that happens to look like one.",
    "under_the_hood": "Expect arenas or malloc with discipline, a retained-mode tree, and immediate-mode temptation. JavaScript integration, if any, is the dangerous part \u2014 usually a bolted-on interpreter or none at all.",
    "why_they_built_it": "Because the conventional stack felt like cheating, or because they wanted to see the bottom.",
    "what_was_difficult": "Text layout, clipping, input focus, and not leaking the tree.",
    "performance": "The available sources do not establish this.",
    "trade_offs": "Total control, total responsibility. Portability and accessibility are usually the first casualties.",
    "what_is_surprising": "It works far enough to shame anyone who said C was the wrong tool. It is still the wrong tool.",
    "quick_read": "A UI in C. The joke is the premise. The value is the engine.",
